---

<ChapterLayout
  title="5.4 — Life on Other Planets · AST 100"
  description="The habitable zone around every kind of star, the rocky worlds we have found inside it, and how starlight filtered through a planet's air could — one day — betray the presence of life."
  activeId="5.4" chapter={5} current="/chapter/5/5.4">
  <Hero
    tease="We now know of more than six thousand planets, and we can read the air of a few of them by the light that passes through it. So: is anyone out there? The honest answer, in 2026, is that we have found a few dozen rocky worlds in the right place, no confirmed sign of life on any of them, and — for the first time in history — the instruments that could actually tell the difference."
    client:load />

  <article class="topic-page">
    <div class="prose-cosmic lesson-prose">
      <h2 id="zone">The Zone Where Water Survives</h2>
      <p>
        Everything in <a href={subPath("5.3")}>§5.3</a> — the vents, the protocells, the whole road to a cell — needed
        one thing above all: <strong>liquid water</strong>. Water is an outstanding solvent; it lets molecules find one
        another and react without falling apart. We cannot prove that life <em>must</em> use it, but we have exactly one
        example of life, and it is built around water. So the search starts there.
      </p>
      <p>
        Put a planet too close to its star and its water boils away into space. Too far, and it freezes solid. Between
        those two disasters lies a band astronomers call the <strong>habitable zone</strong> — sometimes, less formally,
        the Goldilocks zone. Around our Sun it stretches roughly from <strong>0.95 to 1.67 astronomical units</strong>
        (an astronomical unit being the Earth–Sun distance). Earth sits comfortably inside. Venus lies just inside the
        hot edge — and ran away into a suffocating greenhouse. Mars sits out near the cold edge — and froze. The zone is
        not a technicality; our two nearest neighbours are the cautionary tales.
      </p>
      <p>
        The zone's position depends entirely on the star, and stars vary enormously
        (<a href={subPath("3.2")}>§3.2</a>). Step through the star types in the figure below with the arrow keys and
        watch the green band slide inward and shrink as the star dims.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="The habitable zone around different stars">
        <HabitableZonePanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <p>
        This produces a genuinely awkward trade-off, and it is one of the most interesting arguments in modern
        astrobiology. Sun-like <strong>G stars</strong> are hospitable — wide zone, steady light — but they are only
        about seven in a hundred stars, and they only last ten billion years. <strong>Red dwarf (M) stars</strong> are
        the opposite: about <em>seventy</em> in a hundred stars are red dwarfs, and they burn so frugally that they live
        for hundreds of billions of years. No red dwarf anywhere has ever died of old age, because the Universe is not
        yet old enough. Life around one would have essentially unlimited time.
      </p>
      <p>
        But their habitable zone is squeezed right up against the star, and that is a rough neighbourhood. A planet
        there is scorched by flares — Proxima Centauri b, our nearest neighbour world at 4.24 light-years, takes a few
        hundred times Earth's X-ray dose — and it is probably <strong>tidally locked</strong>, one face in permanent
        day, the other in permanent night. Whether an atmosphere can survive that, and spread the heat around, is an
        open question. In between sit the orange <strong>K stars</strong>, dim but calm and very long-lived, which is
        why a growing number of astronomers think they, not stars like ours, are the best place to look.
      </p>
      <p>
        So how many candidates do we have? Of the <strong>6,300-plus</strong> confirmed planets from
        <a href={subPath("4.4")}>§4.4</a>, only a few dozen are roughly Earth-sized worlds sitting in their star's
        habitable zone — the <strong>TRAPPIST-1</strong> system, forty light-years away, contributes several of them, and
        Proxima b is the closest. Every one of them is a <em>candidate</em>. We do not know whether a single one has an
        atmosphere, let alone an ocean. In fact, when JWST looked hard at the innermost TRAPPIST-1 planets in 2025, they
        appear to be bare rock with little or no air at all.
      </p>

      <h2 id="biosignatures">Reading the Air of Another World</h2>
      <p>
        Which brings us to the only tool that could actually settle the question from here. When a planet transits its
        star (<a href={subPath("4.4")}>§4.4</a>), a thin sliver of starlight grazes through the planet's atmosphere on
        its way to us. Every molecule in that air absorbs its own particular wavelengths — its
        <strong>spectral fingerprint</strong>, the same principle by which we read the composition of stars themselves
        (<a href={subPath("0.4")}>§0.4</a>). So the starlight arrives with bites taken out of it, and the pattern of the
        bites tells us what the planet's air is made of. This is <strong>transmission spectroscopy</strong>, and JWST is
        the first telescope good enough to do it on small, rocky worlds.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now, which gases would actually mean something? Toggle them in and out of the atmosphere in the figure below —
        the arrow keys walk the list and the same key adds or removes each gas.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="Reading a planet's atmosphere">
        <SpectrumPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <p>
        Water and carbon dioxide prove nothing: they are everywhere, on dead worlds and live ones alike. The gas that
        matters is <strong>oxygen</strong> — and the reason is chemistry, not biology. Oxygen is so reactive that it
        scrubs itself out of an atmosphere in a geological instant, binding to rocks and gases. If a planet's air is
        full of it, something must be <em>continuously replenishing</em> it. On Earth, that something is life
        (<a href={subPath("5.2")}>§5.2</a>). Better still is the combination of <strong>oxygen and methane</strong>
        together: those two destroy each other on contact, so finding both means both are being pumped out faster than
        they can react away. That state — chemical <strong>disequilibrium</strong> — is the classic
        <strong>biosignature</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        But it is not proof, and this is where a course like this one has a duty to be careful. Non-living processes can
        counterfeit these signals: ultraviolet light splitting water can build up oxygen on a planet with no life
        whatsoever. And the field has just given us a textbook lesson in caution. In 2025 a team reported a possible
        trace of dimethyl sulfide — a gas produced on Earth mainly by marine microbes — in the atmosphere of the
        planet <strong>K2-18 b</strong>. Headlines around the world announced a hint of alien life. Independent teams
        then re-analysed the very same data and found the signal was not statistically significant; it may not be there
        at all. That is not a failure of science. That <em>is</em> science — and it is exactly the process that will have
        to be survived by any future claim.
      </p>
      <p>
        So here is where we honestly stand, in 2026. We have found thousands of worlds. A few dozen of them are rocky
        and sit in the right place. Not one shred of confirmed evidence exists that any of them is alive — every
        candidate so far, from methane on Mars to phosphine on Venus to this year's dimethyl sulfide, has been
        withdrawn, contested, or explained without life. The search is real, the instruments finally exist, and the
        answer is still no. What that silence <em>means</em> is a question we will return to, with far more force, in
        <a href={subPath("6.4")}>§6.4</a> and <a href={subPath("7.4")}>§7.4</a>.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <p><em>Water needs a zone; oxygen needs an explanation; and every claim needs to survive the people trying to
        knock it down. We have the telescope, the method, and the targets — and, so far, an empty result. Meanwhile,
        on the one planet we know is inhabited, that single cell was busy becoming everything else.</em></p>
    </div>
  </article>

